        <div id="certifications-explained" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. NSF Certified for Sport vs Informed Sport vs Informed Choice
          </h2>

          <p>
            NSF Certified for Sport is the program most often required or recommended in North American professional and collegiate sport; NSF says it is recognized by several major leagues, including Major League Baseball, the NFL and the NHL. Certification involves testing products for a list of roughly 280 or more substances banned by major athletic organizations, verifying that the label matches the contents, screening for contaminants and auditing the manufacturing facility, with ongoing retesting. Certified products and lots can be searched on NSF's website and app.
          </p>

          <p>
            Informed Sport, run by the anti-doping laboratory LGC, is especially common in the UK, Europe and Australia and in many Olympic sports. Its defining feature is that every batch of a certified product is tested for banned substances before it is released to market, and athletes can look up the batch number on the tub in a public registry. Informed Choice is its sister program: products are tested for the same banned substances, but by regular sampling rather than every batch before release. Both use the same lab; the difference is frequency.
          </p>

          <p>
            Which one should you use? The answer is whichever your sport, team or federation recognizes. Many US college athletic departments and leagues point athletes to NSF Certified for Sport. Many international federations and national anti-doping agencies refer athletes to Informed Sport or similar batch-testing schemes. If your organization names a program, follow it; if it names none, either NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport is a strong standard, and Informed Choice is a step below for tested athletes.
          </p>

          <p>
            You may also see other marks, such as BSCG Certified Drug Free or listing on the Cologne List. These involve banned-substance testing too, with differing scope and frequency. Be wary of vague phrases like lab tested, third-party tested or banned-substance free without a named certifier you can check. Those may be true, but they are not something you can verify, and they will not help you if a test comes back positive.
          </p>

