        <div id="how-much-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Protein Athletes Need, per the ISSN
          </h2>

          <p>
            The International Society of Sports Nutrition's position stand on protein and exercise recommends 1.4 to 2.0 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day for most exercising people who want to build and maintain muscle. The joint position of the American College of Sports Medicine and North American dietetic organizations gives a similar range of 1.2 to 2.0 g per kilogram for athletes. For a 75 kg athlete, that is roughly 105 to 150 g a day, and more than many athletes eat without planning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where you sit in that range depends on your sport and phase. Endurance athletes in normal training can often thrive near the lower end, while strength and power athletes, and anyone in heavy training blocks, tend to benefit from the upper half. A pooled analysis of training studies found that gains in muscle mass from extra protein largely plateau around 1.6 g per kilogram, with an upper confidence limit near 2.2 g, which is why 1.6 to 2.2 g is a common practical target for strength athletes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dieting changes the picture. The ISSN notes that resistance-trained athletes on a calorie deficit may need 2.3 to 3.1 g per kilogram of lean body mass to protect muscle, which is relevant to weight-class athletes making weight and physique competitors preparing for a show. Higher intakes are generally safe for healthy people; the ISSN's review found no evidence of harm to healthy kidneys at these levels. Anyone with kidney disease should follow their doctor's guidance instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Powder is a tool for closing the gap between what you eat and that target, not the target itself. Most athletes need one or two shakes a day at most. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide and the <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> will give you a personal number in grams, and the <a href="/best-high-protein-powder/">best high protein powder</a> guide covers powders that pack the most into a scoop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dose-and-timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Dose, Timing and Leucine per Meal
          </h2>

          <p>
            The ISSN recommends about 0.25 g of high-quality protein per kilogram of body weight per serving, which works out to roughly 20 to 40 g for most athletes. That dose should contain about 700 to 3,000 mg of leucine, the amino acid most closely tied to switching on muscle protein synthesis. A 25 g scoop of whey isolate supplies around 2.5 to 3 g of leucine, which is why whey is the standard post-training protein. Older athletes, roughly over 50 to 60, appear to need a larger dose, closer to 0.4 g per kilogram, to get the same response.
          </p>

          <p>
            Distribution matters more than precise timing. Spreading protein across the day in three to five feedings of that size, every three to four hours, supports muscle protein synthesis better than one or two very large meals. A protein feeding within a couple of hours either side of training is sensible and easy to arrange, but the strict 30-minute anabolic window has not held up. If you trained fasted early in the morning, getting protein in soon afterwards matters more than if you ate a solid meal before training. Our explainer on <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> covers the timing research in full.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pre-sleep protein is one timing strategy with reasonable support for athletes. The ISSN's nutrient timing position concluded that 30 to 40 g of casein before sleep can increase overnight muscle protein synthesis and metabolic rate without impairing fat breakdown. Much of the long-term benefit appears to come from the extra protein rather than the timing itself, but for athletes who struggle to hit their daily target, a bedtime casein shake is an easy win. Our guide to the <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> covers the options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fast or slow? Whey digests quickly and suits post-training and morning use. Casein digests slowly and suits bedtime and long gaps. Plant proteins are slightly lower in leucine, so plan on 30 g or more per serving to match whey. Ready-to-drink shakes and bars are useful when travelling, but check that they carry the same certification as your powder; many popular ones do not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strength-vs-endurance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Strength vs Endurance Athletes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Strength and power athletes, including lifters, throwers, sprinters and most team-sport players in the weight room, have the simplest protein brief: hit 1.6 to 2.2 g per kilogram, spread it across four or so feedings, and take a 20 to 40 g dose of fast protein around training. Whey isolate is the obvious workhorse. Creatine, not protein, is the supplement with the biggest additional performance effect for this group, and it too should be certified if you are tested.
          </p>

          <p>
            Endurance athletes need protein for different reasons: repairing muscle damaged by long sessions, supporting adaptations such as new mitochondria, and protecting muscle during high training volumes when energy intake can lag behind. Their daily needs typically fall around 1.2 to 1.6 g per kilogram, rising in heavy training blocks. The bigger nutritional priority for endurance work is still carbohydrate, and protein should not crowd it out of the plate.
          </p>

          <p>
            After long or hard endurance sessions, combining carbohydrate with protein is the standard approach. When carbohydrate intake after exercise is lower than ideal, adding protein, often at about a 3 to 4 parts carbohydrate to 1 part protein ratio, can help restore muscle glycogen and supports repair. A shake made with whey isolate and a banana, or a certified recovery formula, covers this neatly. If you have less than a day before the next session, recovery nutrition matters more than if you have two days off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixed-demand athletes, such as soccer, rugby, basketball and hockey players, sit between the two. They generally do well at 1.6 to 2.0 g per kilogram with a protein feeding after training and matches and another before bed during congested schedules. For all groups, the certification requirement does not change: if you are tested, every product in your routine, including sports drinks, gels and recovery shakes, should be on a certifier's list.
          </p>

