                For drug-tested athletes, Thorne Whey Protein Isolate is our top pick: about 21 g of whey isolate per scoop with NSF Certified for Sport status. Klean Isolate is the best unflavored certified option, NOW Sports Whey Isolate the best value with Informed Sport, and Garden of Life Sport the certified plant-based choice. Verify each in the certifier's database first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both screen products for substances banned in sport. NSF Certified for Sport also verifies label claims, screens for contaminants and audits facilities, and is common in US leagues and colleges. Informed Sport tests every batch before release and is common in Europe and Olympic sport. Use whichever program your sport or organization recognizes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Informed Choice good enough for tested athletes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a step below. Informed Choice products are tested for the same banned substances as Informed Sport, but by regular sampling rather than every batch before release. For recreational and untested athletes it is a useful check. Athletes in drug-tested sport should prefer NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, or follow their organization's list.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein do athletes need per day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The ISSN recommends 1.4 to 2.0 g per kilogram of body weight per day for most exercising people, in servings of about 0.25 g per kilogram, or 20 to 40 g. Strength athletes often target 1.6 to 2.2 g per kilogram, endurance athletes around 1.2 to 1.6 g, and athletes dieting hard may need more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a protein powder cause a failed drug test?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, although protein powders are lower risk than pre-workouts and products with anabolic claims. Studies have found undeclared steroids and stimulants in a meaningful share of supplements, and anti-doping rules make athletes strictly liable for what is in their sample. Certified products dramatically reduce the risk, but no program can guarantee zero risk.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
